%[text] # Create Liquid Cooled Data Center Model
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] This example shows how to create a liquid-cooled data center using the composite libraries provided in the project. A few power distribution unit (PDU) racks are combined with one cooling distribution unit (CDU) to form a PDU+CDU assembly. Such an assembly is represented by the **PDU + CDU Assembly (LUT)**, **PDU + CDU Assembly (TL)**, and **PDU + CDU Assembly (TL-TL)** from the Simscape Custom Components library.
%[text] ![](text:image:531e)
%[text] ## Select Composite Library Block
%[text] A large data center can be visualized as a collection of multiple PDU+CDU assembly units. The total number of units is determined by the data center power rating. To determine the data center power rating, click `Get Rating` on any **PDU+CDU Assembly** block variant. The **PDU + CDU Assembly (LUT)** block is suitable for long-duration analysis and for simulating large system models. In this workflow, all IT trays operate at the same server utilization values.
%[text] ## Parameterize PDU
%[text] Specify the parameters required on the three tabs of the block mask: `IT Tray`, `GPU`, and `CPU`. The IT tray mass and specific heat are estimated from the material properties and tray dimensions. The power losses for the GPU, CPU, and other electrical components on the IT tray are typically found in the data sheets for these components. The values used in this example are shown in the following table.
setServerNamePlateParameters;
modelParam.PDU.NPVtbl.dataTbl = dataTbl;
disp(modelParam.PDU.NPVtbl.dataTbl); %[output:3c2ef34b]
clear dataTbl
%[text] The thermal resistance paths from the IT tray to the cold plate and to the ambient are calculated from the physical dimensions and material properties of the components used.
%[text] Specify the number of gpu and cpu used on each IT tray.
modelParam.PDU.NumGPU = 4;
modelParam.PDU.NumCPU = 2;
%[text] Specify the number of IT trays per rack.
modelParam.PDU.NumTrays = 5;
%[text] Specify the number of racks in a PDU unit.
modelParam.PDU.NumRacks = 3;
%[text] ## Parameterize CDU
%[text] One cooling distribution unit (CDU) is packaged with the PDU racks for thermal management. A typical CDU data sheet includes information such as the number of CDU pumps, the heat capacity (in kW), the CDU dimensions, the coolant type, and the noise levels. A typical CDU data sheet is shown in the following figure.
%[text] ![](text:image:4ea4)
%[text] The data from this plot is digitized and used to specify the **Coolant volumetric flowrate vector (m^3/s)**, **Heat exchange capacity vector (W)**, **Pump efficiency vector, 0-1 (-)**, and **Pressure drop (Pa)** parameters on the `CDU` tab of the **PDU + CDU Assembly (LUT)** block mask. The digitized data is shown below.
modelParam.CDU.flowrate_lpm = [18, 30, 60, 78, 102, 120, 156, 198, 210, 222];
modelParam.CDU.Capacity_kW  = [10, 15, 45, 70, 110, 150, 250, 400, 450, 500];
modelParam.CDU.PumpEff_pc   = [40.0, 42.0, 47.0, 50.0, 53.5, 56.0, 60.0, 63.7, 64.0, 63.5];
modelParam.CDU.PrDrop_kPa   = [9, 25, 100, 169, 289, 400, 676, 1089, 1225, 1369;...
                               19.3, 53.7, 215, 363.3, 621.4, 860, 1453.4, 2341.3, 2633.8, 2943.3];
%[text] All the parameter vectors listed above must be the same size as the flow rate parameter vector specified on the block mask. This completes the block setup for thermal analysis. The next section is relevant for adding features for power systems and grid integration analysis.
%[text] ## Specify Assembly Count
%[text] The PDU racks and CDU unit must be replicated to create a large data center model. Use the `getPDUCountForRating` function to estimate the number of times the PDU racks and CDU must be replicated to achieve the desired data center rating. Specify the desired data center rating.
modelParam.DataCenterRating = simscape.Value(500,"kW");
%[text] Calculate number of assemblies required.
modelParam.NumAssemblies = getPDUCountForRating(DataCenterRating=modelParam.DataCenterRating,...
                                                NumOfGPUperTray=modelParam.PDU.NumGPU,...
                                                NumOfCPUperTray=modelParam.PDU.NumCPU,...
                                                NumOfRacksPerPDU=modelParam.PDU.NumRacks,...
                                                NumOfTraysPerRack=modelParam.PDU.NumTrays,...
                                                GPUtdp=simscape.Value(modelParam.PDU.NPVtbl.dataTbl.GPU("TDP per Die [W]"),"W"),...
                                                CPUtdp=simscape.Value(modelParam.PDU.NPVtbl.dataTbl.CPU("TDP per Die [W]"),"W"),...
                                                GPUidlePower=simscape.Value(modelParam.PDU.NPVtbl.dataTbl.GPU("Idle Power [W]"),"W"),...
                                                CPUidlePower=simscape.Value(modelParam.PDU.NPVtbl.dataTbl.CPU("Idle Power [W]"),"W"));
disp(strcat("Number of Assemblies = ",num2str(ceil(modelParam.NumAssemblies)))); %[output:99246141]
%[text] ## Add Electrical Libraries
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_7839] The **PDU + CDU Assembly (LUT)** block acts as a load block for thermal and electrical power consumption in the data center. The *P* port outputs the electrical power consumption of the data center. Connect the *P* port to a **Dynamic Load** block from the Simscape Electrical™ library. This completes the basic electrical and thermal model for a DC architecture data center. For more information about electrical modeling techniques, see:
%[text] - [Stability Assessment of Data Center Power Delivery](file:../Electrical/StabilityAssessment.m) for AC architecture.
%[text] - [Design Solid State Transformer Power Delivery for Hyperscale Data Center](file:../Electrical/HyperscaleSSTDesign.m) for DC Hyperscale architecture. \
%%
%[text] ## Setup Test Harness and Simulate
modelParam.Name = "CreateLiquidCooledDataCenter";
%[text] Open model and simulate.
open_system(modelParam.Name);
sim(modelParam.Name);
%[text] Close model.
close_system(modelParam.Name,1);
%%
%[text] ## Analyse Results
%[text] Load the tabular data from the simulation results.
modelRes.Tbl = logsout_CreateLiqCooledDataCenter.extractTimetable;

figure("Name","Utilization"); %[output:9f476168]
plot(hours(modelRes.Tbl.Time),modelRes.Tbl.("U")); %[output:9f476168]
ylabel("Server Utilization (-)"); %[output:9f476168]
xlabel("Time (hr)"); %[output:9f476168]

figure("Name","Temperature"); %[output:667ea7b6]
plot(hours(modelRes.Tbl.Time),modelRes.Tbl.("T.GPU")); %[output:667ea7b6]
hold on; %[output:667ea7b6]
plot(hours(modelRes.Tbl.Time),modelRes.Tbl.("T.CPU")); %[output:667ea7b6]
hold off %[output:667ea7b6]
ylabel("Temperature (K)"); %[output:667ea7b6]
legend("GPU","CPU"); %[output:667ea7b6]
xlabel("Time (hr)"); %[output:667ea7b6]

